## Reference 
###  `useDebugValue(value, format?)`
Call `useDebugValue` at the top level of your [custom Hook](https://18.react.dev/learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks) to display a readable debug value:
```


import { useDebugValue } from 'react';





function useOnlineStatus() {



  // ...



  useDebugValue(isOnline ? 'Online' : 'Offline');



  // ...



```

[See more examples below.](https://18.react.dev/reference/react/useDebugValue#usage)
#### Parameters 
  * `value`: The value you want to display in React DevTools. It can have any type.
  * **optional** `format`: A formatting function. When the component is inspected, React DevTools will call the formatting function with the `value` as the argument, and then display the returned formatted value (which may have any type). If you don’t specify the formatting function, the original `value` itself will be displayed.


#### Returns 
`useDebugValue` does not return anything.
## Usage 
### Adding a label to a custom Hook 
Call `useDebugValue` at the top level of your [custom Hook](https://18.react.dev/learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks) to display a readable debug value for [React DevTools.](https://18.react.dev/learn/react-developer-tools)
```


import { useDebugValue } from 'react';





function useOnlineStatus() {



  // ...



  useDebugValue(isOnline ? 'Online' : 'Offline');



  // ...



```

This gives components calling `useOnlineStatus` a label like `OnlineStatus: "Online"` when you inspect them:
Without the `useDebugValue` call, only the underlying data (in this example, `true`) would be displayed.
