## Only call Hooks at the top level 
Functions whose names start with `use` are called [_Hooks_](https://18.react.dev/reference/react) in React.
**Don’t call Hooks inside loops, conditions, nested functions, or`try` /`catch`/`finally` blocks.** Instead, always use Hooks at the top level of your React function, before any early returns. You can only call Hooks while React is rendering a function component:
  * ✅ Call them at the top level in the body of a [function component](https://18.react.dev/learn/your-first-component).
  * ✅ Call them at the top level in the body of a [custom Hook](https://18.react.dev/learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks).


```


function Counter() {



  // ✅ Good: top-level in a function component



  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);



  // ...






function useWindowWidth() {



  // ✅ Good: top-level in a custom Hook



  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);



  // ...



```

